<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Keladi Nayaka kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1499 CE – 1763 CE (approx.) · capital Ikkeri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kannada kingdom of the western Ghats ruled from Ikkeri and Bednore, famous for its pepper trade and for Queen Chennamma's defiance of Aurangzeb.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas">Keladi Nayakas</a> began as local chiefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> hills, serving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> as governors from around 1499. After Vijayanagara's defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Talikota</a> in 1565 they became effectively independent, moving their capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> and later, around 1639, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Bidanur</a> (Bednore).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivappa-nayaka">Shivappa Nayaka</a>, the greatest ruler, introduced a celebrated land revenue system, the shist, and expanded the kingdom to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanara">Kanara</a>, driving the Portuguese from several ports. The kingdom profited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> exports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-chennamma">Keladi Chennamma</a>, who ruled from 1671, sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaram-i">Rajaram</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghoreshwara-temple">Aghoreshwara temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> shows the dynasty's patronage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Bednore</a> in 1763, seizing its treasury, and renamed it Haidarnagar. The territory remained part of Mysore and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British India</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/keladi-nayaka-kingdom.jpg" alt="Keladi Nayaka kingdom" width="320" height="396"><figcaption>Image: H. SHASHIDHAR of the Indian Administrative Service and N. SREENIVASA MURTHY Geographer of the DCD, Karnataka. … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greatest_Extent_Of_Keladi_Kingdom_during_the_reign_of_Shivappa_Nayaka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1499 CE – 1763 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ikkeri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shivappa Nayaka, Keladi Chennamma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Lingayat and Vaishnava)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kannada</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Keladi Nayakas, Ikkeri Nayakas, Kingdom of Bednore</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1631&amp;focus=keladi-nayaka-kingdom" aria-label="Keladi Nayaka kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6734 1651 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Keladi Nayaka kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7054 1907L7088 1864M7054 1907L7107 1971"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7088 1864h0M7107 1971h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7054 1907h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7054 1907h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ikkeri. <a href="/atlas?year=1631&amp;focus=keladi-nayaka-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

